    shortTitle: "The dead sheep and the rubbish heap",
    formalTitle: "The censure of the world: first reports from the Prophet",
    overview: "Ghazali says most of the Quran censures the world, so he gives reports instead. The Prophet held up a dead sheep and a rubbish heap as pictures of the world. He called it a prison for the believer. He said love of it is the head of every sin. Abu Bakr wept over a cup of honey and water because of something the Prophet had pushed away.",
    reflection: "Next time you enjoy something sweet, remember Abu Bakr's cup. Ask what the drink is for, and whether you could do without it.",
    relatedNodes: ["dunya", "hubb-dunya", "akhira"],
    thesis: "The reports agree that the world is worth less to God than a dead sheep to its owners. Loving it harms the next life, and loving the next life costs something here. The wise choose what lasts over what passes.",
    context: "The first part of the book is a collection of reports. Ghazali says the Quran's censure of the world is too plain to need quoting, since the prophets were sent for nothing else.",
    moves: [
      { title: "Why reports, not verses", body: "Ghazali says the verses censuring the world are many. Most of the Quran censures the world, turns people away from it and calls them to the next life. That was the very purpose of the prophets; they were sent for nothing else. So he does not need to quote verses, since they are plain. He gives some of the reports instead." },
      { title: "The dead sheep", body: "The messenger of God passed a dead sheep. He asked: “Do you think this sheep was worthless to its owners?” They said: “It was so worthless they threw it away.” He said: “By the One who holds my soul, the world is more worthless to God than this sheep was to its owners. If the world were worth a gnat's wing to God, He would not give an unbeliever a drink of water from it.”" },
      { title: "Prison, curse and choice", body: "The Prophet said: “The world is the believer's prison and the unbeliever's garden.” He said: “The world is cursed, and what is in it is cursed, except what is for God.” Abu Musa al-Ashari reported him saying: “Whoever loves his world harms his next life. Whoever loves his next life harms his world. So prefer what lasts to what passes.” And he said: “Love of the world is the head of every sin.”" },
      { title: "Abu Bakr's cup", body: "Zayd ibn Arqam said they were with Abu Bakr when he asked for a drink. He was brought water and honey. As he raised it to his mouth he wept, until his companions wept too. They fell silent, but he did not. He wept again, until they thought they could not ask him. Then he wiped his eyes. They asked: “Successor of the messenger of God, why did you weep?” He said: “I was with the messenger of God, and I saw him pushing something away from himself. I saw no one with him. I asked what he was pushing away. He said: This is the world. It took a shape before me and I said to it: Get away from me. It came back and said: If you escape me, those after you will not.”" },
      { title: "The rubbish heap", body: "The Prophet said: “How strange, how very strange, is the one who believes in the home that lasts for ever, yet works for the home of delusion!” Once he stood at a rubbish heap and said: “Come and see the world.” He picked up rags that had rotted there and bones that had crumbled, and said: “This is the world.” Ghazali says this points to how the world's finery will wear out like those rags. The bodies that enjoy it will become worn-out bones." },
      { title: "Sweet and green", body: "The Prophet said: “The world is sweet and green. God has made you successors in it, and He is watching how you act. When the world was spread out for the Children of Israel, they lost themselves in jewellery, women, perfume and clothes.” Jesus said: “Do not take the world as a lord, or it will take you as slaves. Store your treasure with the One who will not lose it. The owner of a worldly treasure fears for it. The owner of God's treasure does not.”" },
      { title: "Jesus turns the world face down", body: "Jesus also said: “Disciples, I have thrown the world face down for you, so do not lift it up after me. Part of the world's filth is that God is disobeyed in it. Part of its filth is that the next life is only reached by leaving it. So cross the world; do not build it up. Love of the world is the root of every sin. Many an hour's desire has left a long sorrow behind.” He said: “The world has been laid flat for you, and you sit on its back. Do not let kings and women fight you for it. Do not compete with kings for the world; they will not bother you if you leave them their world. As for women, guard against them with fasting and prayer.”" },
      { title: "The seeker and the sought", body: "Jesus said: “The world seeks and is sought. The world seeks the one who seeks the next life, until he has received all his provision in it. The next life seeks the one who seeks the world, until death comes and takes him by the neck.” Musa ibn Yasar reported the Prophet saying: “God created nothing more hateful to Him than the world. He has not looked at it since He created it.”" },
      { title: "Solomon and the worshipper", body: "Solomon son of David once rode by in his procession. Birds shaded him, and jinn and people walked on his right and left. He passed a worshipper from the Children of Israel, who said: “By God, son of David, God has given you a mighty kingdom!” Solomon heard and said: “One glorification of God in a believer's record is better than what the son of David has been given. What the son of David has will go. The glorification remains.”" },
    ],
    closeReading: [
      { title: "What the Prophet pushed away", body: "Abu Bakr's tears over a sweet drink come from one scene. The world took a shape and came to the Prophet, and he pushed it off twice. Its last words were a threat to everyone who came after him." },
      { title: "One glorification", body: "Solomon had the greatest kingdom any prophet held. His own verdict on it was that a single “Glory be to God” outweighs it all, because the kingdom goes and the glorification stays." },
    ],
    distinction: { title: "Two lovers", firstLabel: "Whoever loves his world", first: "Harms his next life.", secondLabel: "Whoever loves his next life", second: "Pays some cost in this world, and chooses what lasts." },
